 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/partition-labels/",
 "pattern": "Greedy: stretch the part to each letter's last occurrence",
 "statement": "Cut the string `s` into as **many parts as possible** so that each letter appears in at most one part. Return the sizes of the parts, in order.",
 "example": {
  "input": "s = \"abacdcee\"",
  "output": "[3,3,2]",
  "why": "\"aba\" | \"cdc\" | \"ee\" — no letter occurs in two different parts."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "s = \"ababcbacadefegdehijhklij\"",
   "output": "[9,7,8]"
  },
  {
   "input": "s = \"eccbbbbdec\"",
   "output": "[10]"
  },
  {
   "input": "s = \"abc\"",
   "output": "[1,1,1]"
  },
  {
   "input": "s = \"a\"",
   "output": "[1]"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "If a part contains a letter, it must contain **all** occurrences of that letter — in particular the last one.",
  "So first record the **last index** of every letter. Then scan from the left with a current part `start … end`: each letter met pushes `end` out to that letter's last index (if it lies further).",
  "When the scan position reaches `end`, every letter inside the part ends inside it. Cutting here is safe, and cutting as early as possible gives the most parts."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def partitionLabels(self, s: str) -> List[int]:\n        last_index = {}\n\n        for i, char in enumerate(s):\n            last_index[char] = i\n\n        result = []\n        start, end = 0, 0\n\n        for i, char in enumerate(s):\n            end = max(end, last_index[char])\n\n            if i == end:\n                result.append(end - start + 1)\n                start = end + 1\n\n        return result",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(n)",
  "timeWhy": "Two passes over the string.",
  "space": "O(1)",
  "spaceWhy": "At most 26 entries in the last-index map (not counting the output)."
 },
